  "summary": "A headless, deck-of-cards-sized Wi-Fi and BLE recon box with two radios doing two jobs: an ESP32-C5-WROOM-1 handles all dual-band 2.4 and 5 GHz scanning and attacking, while a separate ESP32-WROOM-32E holds the Bluetooth link to your phone and stores Evil Portal pages. You drive it entirely from a phone app. It genuinely reaches 5 GHz for scanning, raw PCAP capture and EAPOL/PMKID collection - which most devices claiming a C5 do not.",
  "honest_take": "The dual-MCU split is real engineering rather than a spec-sheet flourish: because one chip never has to stop scanning to talk to your phone, captures do not drop while you use the app. And the 5 GHz support is documented in the vendor's own wiki rather than only on the sales page, which puts it ahead of most of this category. But three claims do not survive checking. 'Dual-band scanning 2.4 and 5 GHz simultaneously' is not possible - the C5 has one radio, and the vendor's own wiki describes it hopping channels one second at a time. 'BLE 5.0 + Classic' is wrong for the ESP32-WROOM-32E, which Espressif's datasheet puts at Bluetooth 4.2. And where 5 GHz stops is quietly undocumented: Evil Portal is restricted to channels 1, 6 and 11, the deauth-plus-EAPOL combo is documented as 2.4 GHz only, and plain 5 GHz deauthentication is neither claimed nor denied anywhere. The firmware is closed, there is no screen, no SD card, no GPS, no IR, no sub-GHz and no NFC - and you cannot buy one today.",
