  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "The Bruce project's purpose-built board: an ESP32-S3 with 16MB flash and 8MB PSRAM, a real CC1101 sub-GHz transceiver, an ST25R3916 NFC front end, an amplified 2.4GHz module, eight IR LEDs for transmit and receive, a GPS header, microSD and an AW9523 I/O expander with Flipper Zero and iButton header compatibility. It is what Bruce looks like when the hardware matches the firmware.",
  "honest_take": "This is the honest answer to 'Bruce has loads of features that need modules' - here they are all on one board, and the ST25R3916 is the same NFC front end the Flipper Zero uses. But it ships as a bare PCBA with no display, no antenna, no GPS and no case, so the $80 sticker is not the finished cost and it is not a device you hand to a beginner. It has been out of stock, which is the recurring story with small-batch community hardware. The 2.4GHz 'jammer' is an amplified module doing protocol spam and constant carrier, not wideband jamming - amplifying it makes it a louder nuisance and a bigger regulatory problem, not a different capability. On the Wi-Fi side it inherits the usual ceiling: deauth only works where management frames are unprotected, so 802.11w/PMF defeats it and WPA3 mandates PMF.",
