  "summary": "A universal serial-protocol bridge: eight IO pins that can speak 1-Wire, I2C, SPI, UART, MIDI and addressable-LED protocols from an interactive terminal, plus a programmable 1-5 V supply with current limiting and a colour LCD that shows what every pin is doing. The modern rewrite of a tool that has been in hardware hackers' bags since 2008.",
  "honest_take": "It is a bench tool for talking to chips, not an attack tool. The genuinely useful part is not the protocol support - a $5 FTDI cable does UART - it is the current-limited programmable supply and the live per-pin voltage display, which between them stop you from destroying the target while you work out what its pinout is. Two caveats for this comparison: its documented protocol modes do not include JTAG, so for JTAG or SWD you want a Tigard, Glasgow or JTAGulator instead; and its IO range starts at 1.2 V, which is better than most, but it is a level-translated bridge rather than a full logic analyser.",
