  "summary": "A pocket multitool built around four independent radios - a CC1101 sub-GHz transceiver, an ST25R3916 13.56 MHz NFC front end, a separate 125 kHz coil driven straight off the STM32, and the STM32WB55's own Bluetooth LE core - plus infrared, iButton and an 18-pin GPIO header. Momentum is the customisation-first community firmware: it removes the sub-GHz region lock, can push the CC1101 beyond its rated bands, and bundles the apps people actually install anyway - BLE Spam, FindMy Flipper, NFC Maker, Wardriver, Bad-Keyboard. It is the direct continuation of Xtreme, written by the same developers.",
  "honest_take": "It is a very well-built fork, but almost everything people credit it with is either an APP that also runs on other firmware, or a hardware capability that was always there and was only being withheld by policy. The extended frequency range (281-361 / 378-481 / 749-962 MHz) is the one to be careful about: it drives the CC1101's PLL outside TI's specified bands and the antenna matching is wrong there, so you get poor performance and the projects themselves warn it can damage the hardware. Also note the release cadence - the last tagged stable is mntm-012 from 2025-12-31 and everything since has been dev builds.",
