  "price_note": "160 USD. Product page read 'Sold out' on 2026-09-06 while the collection page showed in stock; the product page is the safer reading. The v1 board at $146 is also sold out.",
  "price_is_estimate": false,
  "price_checked": "2026-09-06",
  "skill_level": "intermediate",
  "skill_note": "Physically plug-and-play with a Flipper, but you are living in Marauder's menus and you will need to understand which antenna belongs on which SMA.",
  "build_effort": "assembled",
  "build_note": "Accessory: designed for the Flipper Zero GPIO header, though the ESP32 can be driven standalone over USB. Enclosure designed by AWOK Dynamics. Five antennas must be fitted to the correct connectors.",
  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "The 'Big Flipper Board' from the author of ESP32 Marauder: a maximalist Flipper add-on carrying an ESP32-C5 for dual-band Wi-Fi, two separate CC1101 radios individually tuned for the 400 MHz and 900 MHz bands, an nRF24 with PA and LNA, GPS and a microSD slot, with five SMA connectors on the edge. It is the most radio-dense Flipper add-on board that is actually a designed product rather than a generic import.",
  "honest_take": "The two band-tuned CC1101s are the genuinely clever bit - a single CC1101 with one antenna is always a compromise across 300-900 MHz, and splitting it lets you also hang your own amplifier on each band. Everything else is the usual multiboard bill of materials, just done properly. Three practical warnings: it is expensive at $160 and almost never in stock; the Marauder wiki carries a dedicated 'BFFB NRF24 Fix' page, which means at least one revision shipped with an nRF24 hardware problem; and 500 mW into an nRF24 is not a magic weapon - it is still a 2.4 GHz GFSK transceiver, and pushing it that hard is an interference and legality question, not a capability upgrade.",
