  "stealth": "overt",
  "form_factor": "board",
  "summary": "An FPGA-based digital interface multitool that reconfigures its logic for each protocol rather than bit-banging from a microcontroller. It handles UART, SPI, QSPI, I2C, JTAG and SWD, debugs ARM7TDMI, ARC and MIPS cores, reads 24-series I2C EEPROMs and 25-series SPI flash, and from revD upwards includes a logic analyzer. Certified open hardware by OSHWA.",
  "honest_take": "The FPGA is not a gimmick - it is why Glasgow can analyse SPI and QSPI at around 100 MHz and hold precise timing that an FT2232H or an RP2040 cannot. It is also the most technically ambitious tool in this batch and the hardest to buy: production runs in batches, revC is out of stock, and revD has been in pre-launch for a while. If you need something today, get a Tigard. Get a Glasgow if you are doing genuine reverse engineering of unusual or high-speed interfaces and are comfortable in a Python-and-HDL toolchain.",
