  "stealth": "discreet",
  "form_factor": "usb-stick",
  "summary": "The original open-source 2.4 GHz Bluetooth monitoring platform, built on a TI CC2400 radio and an NXP LPC175x ARM Cortex-M3. It made Bluetooth traffic visible to researchers for the first time on affordable hardware. It has been retired by its creator and, for BLE work specifically, has been comprehensively overtaken.",
  "honest_take": "Do not buy this in 2026 unless you specifically need Bluetooth Classic. It is retired, what is on sale is clones, and for BLE a $20 Sonoff dongle running Sniffle beats it on every axis - Sniffle listens on all three advertising channels at once and supports BLE 5's 2M and coded PHYs, none of which the CC2400 can do. Its Classic capability is also more limited than its reputation suggests: the CC2400 can only demodulate 1 Mbps basic-rate GFSK, so EDR (2 and 3 Mbps) traffic is simply invisible to it, and it decrypts nothing. Historically important, practically superseded.",
